<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

A page on a television is read from across a room with a remote in hand. A page on a watch has a few seconds of the
user's attention. A page in a car must not ask for careful aim. A page on an e-ink reader shouldn't animate. These
are different designs, not different sizes.

[[MEDIAQUERIES-5]] describes what a device can do, and a page can guess the kind of device from that: a large
viewport with a fine pointer is probably a computer, and ''update/slow'' suggests e-ink. The guesses are wrong
exactly where it matters. A television reports a large viewport and looks like a desktop, though it is used from
three meters away with a directional pad. A tablet and a touchscreen laptop report the same pointer and hover
capabilities. A car's screen reports nothing that distinguishes it from a tablet.

<div class=example id=example-intro>
    <pre highlight=css>
    /* A television is read from across the room, and focused with a remote */
    @media (form-factor: tv) {
      :root { font-size: 24px; }
      :focus-visible { outline: 4px solid; outline-offset: 4px; }
      .hover-only-toolbar { display: none; }
    }

    /* A watch shows one thing at a time */
    @media (form-factor: watch) {
      .sidebar, .related, .footer-links { display: none; }
      .headline { font-size: 1.1rem; }
    }

    /* A car: bigger targets, less to read, nothing that moves */
    @media (form-factor: automotive) {
      button, a.button { min-block-size: 4rem; font-size: 1.5rem; }
      .marquee { animation: none; }
    }

    /* E-ink: no animation, no gray-on-gray */
    @media (form-factor: eink) {
      * { animation: none !important; transition: none !important; }
      body { color: black; background: white; }
    }
    </pre>
</div>

The kinds of device are the ones that the <code>Sec-CH-UA-Form-Factors</code> client hint already defines
[[UA-CLIENT-HINTS]], with one addition, ''form-factor/tv'', which that hint had and then dropped; see [[#tv]]. Unlike the
platform [[PLATFORM-MEDIA-QUERY]], this feature does reveal something that a site has to ask for today, so
[[#frames]] and [[#reduced-exposure]] limit who gets it.


<h2 id=feature>The '@media/form-factor' feature</h2>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: form-factor
Value: desktop | mobile | tablet | tv | watch | xr | eink | automotive | other
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

The '@media/form-factor' media feature is a [=system media feature=]. A device can have more than one form factor: a
watch is also mobile, and a car's screen can also be a tablet. The feature matches a keyword when that keyword is one
of the document's [=form factors=], rather than testing a single value.

The <dfn export>form factors</dfn> of a [=document=] are the kinds of device it is shown on, each of which is:

: <dfn value for=@media/form-factor>desktop</dfn>
:: A personal computer.
: <dfn value for=@media/form-factor>mobile</dfn>
:: A small, touch-oriented device that the user carries with them.
: <dfn value for=@media/form-factor>tablet</dfn>
:: A touch-oriented device larger than a ''form-factor/mobile'' one, that isn't typically carried on the person.
: <dfn value for=@media/form-factor>tv</dfn>
:: A television or set-top device, shown on a distant screen and usually operated with a remote control.
: <dfn value for=@media/form-factor>watch</dfn>
:: A device worn on the wrist, whose screen is typically less than two inches.
: <dfn value for=@media/form-factor>xr</dfn>
:: An immersive device that augments or replaces the surroundings.
: <dfn value for=@media/form-factor>eink</dfn>
:: A device with a screen that updates slowly and has limited or no color.
: <dfn value for=@media/form-factor>automotive</dfn>
:: A device built into a vehicle, whose user may be driving it and unable to attend to small details.
: <dfn value for=@media/form-factor>other</dfn>
:: A kind of device with no keyword here, or one the user agent chooses not to identify. A document whose form
    factors are empty or unknown has ''form-factor/other'' as its only form factor.

In a [=boolean context=], the feature is always true.

The list of keywords is closed. A user agent must report ''form-factor/other'' for a kind of device with no keyword,
and must not invent keywords. New keywords are added by revising this specification.

<div class=example id=example-multiple>
    On a watch, both of these match, because the device's form factors are ''form-factor/watch'' and
    ''form-factor/mobile'':

    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (form-factor: watch) { … }
    @media (form-factor: mobile) { … }
    </pre>

    A page that wants phones but not watches says so:

    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (form-factor: mobile) and (not (form-factor: watch)) { … }
    </pre>
</div>

<h3 id=new-keywords>Adding keywords</h3>

A keyword added in a later revision is an unknown value to a user agent that implements an earlier one, and a media
query that evaluates to unknown because of it is replaced with ''not all'' [[MEDIAQUERIES-4]]. That includes a query
that negates it, so <code>not (form-factor: projector)</code> would match nothing in such a user agent.

<div class=note>
    A new keyword is safest in its own media query in a comma-separated list, where only that query is dropped:

    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (form-factor: tv), (form-factor: projector) { … }
    </pre>
</div>

A page can't test for a keyword with ''@supports'', but script can:
<code>matchMedia("(form-factor: projector), not all and (form-factor: projector)").matches</code> is true when the
user agent knows the keyword, and false when it doesn't, including when it doesn't support '@media/form-factor' at
all. Keeping the list closed means that each new keyword arrives through a revision that authors can track, rather
than differing between user agents indefinitely.

<h3 id=source>Where the value comes from</h3>

A user agent that implements [[UA-CLIENT-HINTS]] must report the same form factors that it reports in
<code>Sec-CH-UA-Form-Factors</code> and in <code>navigator.userAgentData.getHighEntropyValues(["formFactors"])</code>
for the document, lowercased, plus ''form-factor/tv'' where [[#tv]] applies.

A user agent that doesn't implement those must report the kinds of device that match the definitions above, except
that its form factors must agree with the platform it presents [[PLATFORM-MEDIA-QUERY]], and this takes precedence
over the definitions: if the user agent presents itself as a desktop system, as browsers do in a "request desktop
site" mode, the document's form factors are ''form-factor/desktop'', plus ''form-factor/tv'' where [[#tv]] applies.

<div class=note>
    A user agent that implements the client hint already applies its own presentation to the hint, so the first rule
    covers it.
</div>

<div class=example id=example-desktop-tablets>
    At the time of writing, Safari on iPads other than iPad mini presents itself as macOS by default
    [[WEBKIT-SAFARI-13]], so those iPads report ''form-factor/desktop'' rather than ''form-factor/tablet''. Chrome on
    Android doesn't set the mobile bit on tablet-sized screens, so its client hint reports "Desktop" there
    [[CHROMIUM-UA-UTILS]], and on premium tablets it also presents itself as Linux by default
    [[CHROME-DESKTOP-MODE]]; those tablets report ''form-factor/desktop'' too.
</div>

<div class=note>
    The feature can only be as detailed as the user agent's own classification, and today that is coarse. Only
    Chromium implements <code>Sec-CH-UA-Form-Factors</code>, and by default it reports "Mobile" or "Desktop" from
    the mobile bit, adding "XR" on Android XR devices [[CHROMIUM-UA-UTILS]]. Chromium's own browser reports "Tablet"
    only in ChromeOS's "request tablet site" mode (Android apps that embed its WebView can set any value), and no
    default code path reports "Automotive", "EInk", or "Watch". Together with the desktop presentation of most
    tablets, this means ''form-factor/tablet'' would match almost nowhere in current browsers. The keywords are
    worth defining anyway, so that a user agent that does classify devices has one way to say so, but authors
    should expect ''form-factor/mobile'' and ''form-factor/desktop'' to be most of what they see.
</div>

<h3 id=tv>Televisions</h3>

<code>Sec-CH-UA-Form-Factors</code> has no value for a television, so a television browser that implements it reports
something else; one built on Chromium reports "Mobile" or "Desktop" from the same mobile bit as any other device,
unless its maker changes that [[CHROMIUM-UA-UTILS]]. A user agent must report ''form-factor/tv'' for a television or
set-top device even so.

<div class=note>
    This is the one place where the feature and the client hint can disagree, and it should be fixed in the hint
    rather than here. The hint had a television value: when it was first added, in March 2023, its values were
    "Automobile", "Mobile", "Tablet", "TV", "VR", "XR", and "Unknown" [[UA-CH-338]], and the W3C Media and
    Entertainment Interest Group's issue on how television browsers should identify themselves was updated to note
    that the new hint allowed "TV" [[ME-IG-47]]. In the discussion that followed, "TV" was dropped "in the absence
    of use-cases for it identified so far in this conversation" [[UA-CH-344]], and in September 2023 the hint became
    a list without it [[UA-CH-343]].

    The hint's own criteria for adding a value are "a new form-factor that users interact with in a meaningfully
    different way; a compelling use-case where sites would like to change how they interact with users on that
    device; and no reliable way to identify that new form-factor using existing hints" [[UA-CLIENT-HINTS]]. A
    television meets all three: it is used from across a room with a remote control, [[#intro]] gives the use case,
    and no client hint identifies it. Among media features, only ''pointer/none'' hints at it, and not on
    televisions whose remotes act as pointers. Otherwise, television browsers can often be recognized only from
    unstandardized <code>User-Agent</code> tokens. See [[#open-issues]].
</div>

<h3 id=changes>Changes</h3>

The value is determined when the [=document=] is created, and doesn't change while the document exists.

<h3 id=frames>Frames</h3>

The feature uses [=frame-scoped exposure=] [[!MEDIA-FEATURE-PRIVACY]]. Its policy-controlled feature is
"<code>ch-ua-form-factors</code>", the one that already delegates the client hint [[CLIENT-HINTS-INFRASTRUCTURE]],
whose default allowlist is <code>&#39;self&#39;</code>.

In a document that isn't [=allowed to use=] it, the feature takes its [=restricted value=], which is the
[=coarse form factor=].

<div class=note>
    A site that delegates the client hint to a frame, so that the frame's server can adapt, gives the frame's style
    sheets the same information: <code>Permissions-Policy: ch-ua-form-factors=(self "widgets.example")</code>.
</div>

<h3 id=reduced-exposure>Reduced exposure</h3>

The feature is [=under user control=]. In a [=reduced-exposure context=], and when the user turns the feature off, it
is the [=coarse form factor=].

The <dfn export>coarse form factor</dfn> of a document is ''form-factor/mobile'' if the user agent reports itself as
mobile, in the <code>Sec-CH-UA-Mobile</code> client hint, in <code>navigator.userAgentData.mobile</code>, or with a
"Mobile" token in its <code>User-Agent</code> header, and ''form-factor/desktop'' otherwise.

<div class=note>
    The coarse form factor is the bit that every site already receives on every request, so a document that gets only
    it learns nothing new. A watch or a car falls back to ''form-factor/mobile'', and a television to
    ''form-factor/desktop'', which is what a page assumes today.
</div>


<h2 id=privacy>Privacy considerations</h2>

<h3 id=privacy-exposed>What the feature reveals</h3>

The feature reveals which kinds of device, from a closed list of nine, the page is being shown on. It doesn't reveal
the device's model, manufacturer, screen size, operating system version, or price.

Unlike the platform [[PLATFORM-MEDIA-QUERY]], this is more than every site receives today. Of the signals it
corresponds to, only the mobile bit is sent to every site by default; the rest of
<code>Sec-CH-UA-Form-Factors</code> is a hint a site must ask for, and which isn't shared with cross-origin frames
unless the top-level site delegates it. This specification keeps both of those limits: [[#frames]] uses the same
policy, and [[#reduced-exposure]] falls back to the mobile bit.

<h3 id=privacy-fingerprinting>Fingerprinting</h3>

Most users are on a phone, a tablet, or a computer, so for most users the feature reveals little more than the
mobile bit does. The users it identifies are the ones on uncommon devices: a watch, a headset, an e-ink reader, or a
car. If one user in a thousand browses from a watch, the feature tells a site about 10 bits about that user.

Two things limit the cost:

* Some of those devices are already recognizable without this feature: a watch has a tiny viewport and often round
    safe-area insets, and an e-ink reader reports ''update/slow'' and often ''color/0''. For those, the feature
    mostly saves authors from guessing. A headset would be recognizable from ''environment-blending'', but no
    browser implements that feature yet, and a car has no such signal, so for users of those devices this feature
    does reveal something new.
* The list is closed, so an unusual device reports ''form-factor/other'' rather than naming itself, and the value is
    restricted in frames and in reduced-exposure contexts.

The value never changes while a document exists, so it can't link two documents by the moment it changed.

<h3 id=privacy-third-party>Embedded content</h3>

Cross-origin frames get the [=coarse form factor=] unless the embedding page delegates
<code>ch-ua-form-factors</code> to them, which is exactly the rule the client hint already follows. A tracker
embedded across many sites therefore learns the same bit it already gets from the <code>User-Agent</code> header.

<h3 id=privacy-inference>Sensitive inferences</h3>

A device can suggest things about its owner. A car's browser says the user is in a vehicle; a headset says they are
wearing one; an e-ink reader or a watch suggests what someone bought. None of this is precise, and none of it
approaches what the same page learns from its own content and the user's IP address, but a site can use the value to
decide who is worth serving: to charge differently, or to send an unusable page to devices its author didn't like.
See [[#security-discrimination]].

<h3 id=privacy-residual>Remaining risks</h3>

* A user on an uncommon device is identified by the value in any document the value reaches, which is the top-level
    document and frames that the site delegates to.
* The fallback for reduced-exposure contexts is a real loss of function for those same users: a watch in a private
    window gets the phone layout.


<h2 id=security>Security considerations</h2>

<h3 id=security-not-detection>It is not feature detection</h3>

The kind of device doesn't say which features work, how large the viewport is, or whether there is a pointer. Pages
should keep using '@media/pointer', '@media/hover', '@media/update', '@media/overflow-block',
'@media/environment-blending', the viewport features, and ''@supports'' for those, and use this feature for the
things they can't express: how far away the screen is, how much attention the user can spare, and what kind of
control the user holds.

<h3 id=security-discrimination>Refusing to serve devices</h3>

The clearest misuse is a page that decides some devices don't deserve content: a site that shows a television or an
e-ink reader a "please use a supported device" message rather than a page that works. This has a long history with
user agent sniffing, and it is the reason CSS deprecated its device-class media types. Authors should treat the
feature as a way to adapt, never as a way to exclude, and the fallbacks in this specification are designed so that an
unrecognized device gets a usable layout rather than nothing.

<h3 id=security-automotive>Cars</h3>

''form-factor/automotive'' exists so a page can be safer to use while driving: larger targets, less text, no
animation. A page could do the opposite. Nothing in CSS can prevent that, and vehicle systems restrict what browsers
may show while moving; this feature doesn't change those restrictions, and must not be treated as permission to show
anything.

<h3 id=security-not-a-decision>Not an input to decisions that matter</h3>

The user controls the value: desktop mode changes it, and so does turning the feature off. Pages must not use it for
entitlement, fraud scoring, or any security decision.


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

<h3 id=alt-capabilities>Only capability features</h3>

CSS could keep describing capabilities and let pages infer the rest, adding features for the qualities that are
missing, such as viewing distance or the kind of control the user holds. That is how the CSS Working Group has
handled similar requests, turning "what device is this" into "what does it do", as the Device Posture API did by
exposing ''device-posture/folded'' rather than a device model [[DEVICE-POSTURE]]. The Working Group has open
issues in this direction, for enclosed screens [[CSSWG-9701]] and for typical viewing distance [[CSSWG-13398]].
When the Working Group was asked to expose whether a device is mobile [[CSSWG-7696]], Tab Atkins, an editor of
Media Queries, answered that the media types were deprecated and not reproduced as a media query "precisely
*because* it's an incoherent concept and never what you *actually* want". That issue is still open.

It was not chosen because the missing qualities are many and correlated, and because a user agent is better placed
to classify its device than a page is, which is why <code>Sec-CH-UA-Form-Factors</code> exists. That argument is
weaker than it looks: only Chromium implements the hint, and it classifies little beyond mobile and desktop
([[#source]]). A reviewer may well prefer this direction, and [[#open-issues]] asks the question.

<h3 id=alt-media-types>Reviving the media types</h3>

CSS had <code>@media tv</code>, <code>@media handheld</code>, and <code>@media projection</code>, and deprecated
them, because media types "have proven insufficient as a way of discriminating between devices with different
styling needs" [[MEDIAQUERIES-4]]; user agents must now make them match nothing. Media Queries explains that
categories such as <code>screen</code> and <code>handheld</code> blended over time, and that media types were
mutually exclusive, so a device could be only one of them. Authors also used them for layout, which viewport queries
do better. This is the first objection this feature will meet. The feature is narrower: a device can have several
form factors, capabilities and sizes are left to the features that describe them, and the rules keep an
unrecognized device working.

<h3 id=alt-screen-size>Physical screen size</h3>

A feature for the physical size of the display would separate a television from a laptop, and a watch from a phone.
It was not chosen because it is a continuous value, and therefore a much larger fingerprinting surface than a closed
list of nine keywords, and because '@media/form-factor' answers the question that authors actually ask.

<h3 id=alt-hint-only>Leaving it to the client hint</h3>

Sites can already request <code>Sec-CH-UA-Form-Factors</code>, which Chrome has supported since version 124, and serve
different CSS. That works only in Chromium-based browsers, only for pages
built on a server that varies its response, doesn't work for static files, caches badly, and doesn't work at all
where script and server negotiation aren't available, such as an HTML email or a document opened from disk.


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

<p class=issue>''form-factor/tv'' has no matching value in <code>Sec-CH-UA-Form-Factors</code>, so this
specification requires a value the client hint can't express. The right fix is to add "TV" back to the hint, which
dropped it because no one in that discussion had identified a use case for it ([[#tv]]). Until then, should the feature report
''form-factor/tv'' at all, or wait?

<p class=issue>Should ''form-factor/tablet'' exist? Safari on most iPads presents itself as a desktop computer by
default, Chrome's client hint reports "Desktop" on Android tablets, and Chromium's own browser reports "Tablet" only
in one ChromeOS mode ([[#source]]), so the keyword would match almost nowhere today. Keeping it costs little, and removing it would leave
no way to say "tablet" if user agents start reporting it.

<p class=issue>The CSS Working Group has already been asked for a "mobileness" media query, and participants
including a Media Queries editor pushed back in that issue, which is still open [[CSSWG-7696]]. Does a closed list of device classes answer that objection, or should this proposal become the
capability features that issue and [[CSSWG-13398]] point toward?

<p class=issue>Should ''form-factor/eink'' exist when '@media/update' already reports ''update/slow''? They aren't
the same thing, since a slow-updating LCD isn't e-ink, but they overlap more than the other values do.

<p class=issue>Is matching against a set the right model for a discrete media feature? Every other discrete feature
in [[MEDIAQUERIES-5]] has one value at a time, and a set makes ''(form-factor: mobile)'' true on a watch, which
some authors will not expect.

<p class=issue>Should the restricted value in frames be the [=coarse form factor=], or should a frame see nothing at
all? The coarse value is what the frame's own requests already carry, but it also means a frame always gets an
answer that looks authoritative.


<h2 id=questionnaire class=no-num>Appendix: Security and privacy questionnaire</h2>

*This section is non-normative.* These are the answers to the questions in
[[SECURITY-PRIVACY-QUESTIONNAIRE]].

: What information does this feature expose, and for what purposes?
:: Which kinds of device, from a closed list of nine, the page is shown on, so pages can adapt their design to the
    device rather than guess from its capabilities.
: Do features in your specification expose the minimum amount of information necessary?
:: Yes. A closed list, with no model, size, or version, limited in frames and reduced to the mobile bit where
    fingerprinting protections apply.
: Do the features expose personal information, PII, or information derived from either?
:: The kind of device can suggest what someone owns or that they are in a vehicle. See [[#privacy-inference]].
: How do the features deal with sensitive information?
:: The same limits as the client hint it mirrors, plus a fallback to the bit every site already receives.
: Does data exposed carry related but distinct information that may not be obvious to users?
:: Yes: owning a watch, a headset, or an e-ink reader, and being in a car.
: Do the features introduce state that persists across browsing sessions?
:: No.
: Do the features expose information about the underlying platform to origins?
:: Yes, the device class, which user agents that implement <code>Sec-CH-UA-Form-Factors</code> already compute for
    that hint.
: Does this specification allow an origin to send data to the underlying platform?
:: No.
: Do features enable access to device sensors?
:: No.
: Do features enable new script execution or loading mechanisms?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin to access other devices?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin some measure of control over a user agent's native UI?
:: No.
: What temporary identifiers do the features create or expose to the web?
:: None. The value doesn't change while a document exists.
: How does this specification distinguish between behavior in first-party and third-party contexts?
:: Cross-origin frames get the coarse form factor unless the embedding page delegates
    <code>ch-ua-form-factors</code>, the same rule the client hint follows.
: How do the features work in the context of a browser's Private Browsing or Incognito mode?
:: Modes that protect against fingerprinting are reduced-exposure contexts, where the feature is the coarse form
    factor.
: Does this specification have both "Security Considerations" and "Privacy Considerations" sections?
:: Yes.
: Do features enable origins to downgrade default security protections?
:: No.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature is kept alive in BFCache?
:: It keeps the value it was created with.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature gets disconnected?
:: Nothing is kept.
: Does your spec define when and how new kinds of errors should be raised?
:: No.
: Does your feature allow sites to learn about the user's use of assistive technology?
:: No. A device used as an accessibility aid, such as a switch-controlled tablet, reports the same value as any other
    tablet.
: What should this questionnaire have asked?
:: Whether a feature lets a site decide that some users' devices aren't worth serving.
